Q2.  How does Java enforce Encapsulation?

Encapsulation in Java means bundling data (variables) and methods (functions) together in a class and restricting direct access to the data.
Java enforces encapsulation mainly through access modifiers:-
1. private → accessible only inside the same class.
2. protected → accessible in the same package and subclasses.
3. public → accessible from anywhere.
Default (no modifier) → accessible within the same package.

Example:-
private int age;
public void setAge(int a) {
   age = a;
}

